#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""C++ to Blueprint: a program's ordinary C++, written as Blueprints.

A Blueprint and its C++ are one thing in two notations (kern/docs/blueprint_cpp.md); the notation
is a subset of C++. A game written in ordinary C++ uses more of the language than that subset.
This tool reads such sources with the compiler's own parser (clang's syntax tree) and writes them
in the notation, function by function:

  tools/cpp_to_blueprint.py --convert <namespace> --level <level.klevel> [--project <name.kproject>] --folder <name>
      [--whole-program] [--test <dir>] [--texts <dir>] [--program <folder>=<namespace>[,<namespace>...]]...
      [--classes <header.h>] <include flags...> -- <file.cpp>...

  - the program is the folders of the sources, in the namespace given - and the further folders named
    with --program, each with the namespaces its files declare (vendored code the program includes:
    --program firmware=fc,hwcontract). Their headers are the program's like the sources' own, each a
    Blueprint; their types are named with their namespace (fc::V3 is fc_V3, as net::Session is
    net_Session);

  - every header and every source file becomes a Blueprint (terrain.h -> "terrain", terrain.cpp ->
    "terrain_cpp"), of the project or of an actor of the level (kern/docs/project.md): the program's
    stand in the project (--project) - its App (a state that implements the engine's App), every
    Blueprint with an export, and every Blueprint one of the program uses -, the others, a world's, in
    the level. The log says of each where it stands and why (place_blueprints). Most are libraries - a Blueprint that keeps nothing. Its
    functions are the Blueprint's functions, the constants they use its constants, the types it
    defines the Blueprint's types - whoever computes with them uses it. A header's function that
    needs a source file (it calls what stands in one) stands with the source file of the header's
    name. The types the converter makes of the notation's own (a row of a list of lists, a pair)
    stand in the library "shapes" - or, made of the program's types, with the last of them;
  - a function of another file is called where it stands (common::clampf(v, 0.0, 1.0)): every
    function is written once in the project. Of the headers of the engine, what the program calls
    is written the same way (kern/math/math.h -> "engine_math_math");
  - a type has one name in the whole project, made of its own: the namespaces and classes it
    stands in are part of it (net::Session is net_Session, the engine's physics::Body
    physics_Body) - the engine's nodes name the engine's types the same way;
  - a function is written when everything in it has a place in the notation, and so has what it
    calls; the others stay C++ until the notation (or this tool) has a place for what they use;
  - --test writes, for every file, a test that holds the source of each converted function once
    more, compiled with the numbers and the mathematics of the Blueprint language (f32 is double,
    the functions of numbers are kern::math's), and calls it, the Blueprint in the interpreter and
    the Blueprint as its compiled class with the same arguments: what the three give must be the
    same bit for bit;
  - --texts writes each Blueprint's text into a directory, to read;
  - the Blueprints of the level are the program's sources where they stand outside the folder this tool
    writes (--folder, required: it knows its own by it; --whole-program: a run over the whole program,
    which drops of its folder what it does not write any more): a file that is a Blueprint now - its C++ is gone, the Blueprint in the
    level is its one source (BLUEPRINTS.md, "5 - Umschalten") - stands in a folder of its own. A run
    neither rewrites nor drops such a Blueprint (a run over the whole program drops, of its own folder,
    what it does not write any more). The program's C++ calls it as its class: it is the program's, so it stands
    in the project (kern/docs/project.md) - every Blueprint of the project the run does not write is one the program
    calls as its class (never a folder's name says it). --classes names the header the build writes their classes
    into (tools/blueprint_cpp, the same tool, from the same project; no default: without it the program calls no
    Blueprint as its class). Where the project has such Blueprints, the run writes the header into a folder of its
    own before it reads the sources, and that folder comes first on the include path; a test includes it
    by its name (TestWriter: a header that is not in the sources' folder is the build's);

How the Blueprint computes is the Blueprint language's own arithmetic: numbers are 64-bit and
whole numbers exact, a division by zero gives 0, a vector's components are 32-bit. It is the same
in the interpreter, in the JIT and in the compiled class, on every machine. Where the C++ brings a
number to 32 bit in so many words, so does the Blueprint (the formula's `single`); unsigned 32-bit
arithmetic and bit operations have formula functions of their own. A 64-bit whole number is exact
while it is within 2^53 of zero; an unsigned one is held read with its sign (WIDE_UNSIGNED). A number
literal is the decimal the source spells (0.1f is 0.1).

A 64-bit number the program spells u64, i64, uint64_t or int64_t is a bit pattern - a key made of
two 32-bit numbers, a hash, the state of a random number generator. In the Blueprint it is the
struct Whole64 of its two halves, and what C++ computes with it the functions of
tools/blueprint_whole64.h compute (w64_add, w64_multiply, w64_shift_left ...): C++ of the kind
this tool converts, written as the library "blueprint_whole64" and compared with their source
like the program's own functions. (A number spelled size_t, long ... is one the program counts
with: the Blueprint's whole number, as above.)

A map is the list of its rows - structs of a key (`first`) and what it holds (`second`) -, a set
the list of its keys, in the order the keys were put in (std::map and std::set: in the order of
the keys). An iterator into a map, a set or a list is the place it stands at, a whole number; the
end is the list's length; one that goes through a list from its end (rbegin() ... rend()) is the place
it holds, as C++ defines it: the list's length at first, 0 at the end, the entry it names the one before
that place. `it = v.erase(it)` takes the entry out and leaves the iterator where it stood; `a.swap(b)` of
two lists hands each what the other held (three Takes and Sets, nothing copied). `auto [a, b] = value;` gives names to the parts of a value: where they
stand, the part is written out.

An object of the engine is a handle in a Blueprint (0: none). One a value holds as a field (a
`kern::net::HostTransport transport_;`, a `kern::LightPool lights{16, 96};`) is made where C++ makes the
value: by its constructor (X_new), in the order of the fields, before the constructor's body - what C++
makes of a field itself too, and of the fields of a value inside it -, and it is released where C++ ends
the value, in the reverse order: at the end of the block of a local variable, where what owns such a value
(std::unique_ptr, std::optional) gets another one or is reset. The struct's default is the handle 0. The
one object of a state is made when its Blueprint begins (Begin Play calls its constructor - the one it
declares, also `= default` where it is defined outside the class), before anything else of it: in the order of the fields,
what C++ makes of them - and a field whose initializer is computed, no constant (`T* p[2] = {&a(), &b()};`, a call),
starts at zero and is set there (Unit.computed_init) -, then the constructor's body. What C++ does
with such values that one handle cannot do the same way - a copy, a move or a temporary of one, one handed
in or given back by value, a list or a map of them changed - waits (Emit.lifetimes).

A parameter `const T*` a function reads entries through (`p[i]`, `*p`), hands on (`p + 2`) or asks
whether it is null is a pointer into a list, like an iterator: the list, read where it stands (a const
reference input), and the place in it (an Int; null is -1). Its list is of the type every call of the
program hands it - a std::array<T, N> is Fixed<T, N> - and `a.data() + k` hands (a, k). A pointer of a
function to an entry of a list, or to none (`Car* job = there ? &cars[i] : nullptr;`, never given another
value), is the same: the list where it stands and the place in it; `job->x` is the entry's field, read and
changed there, `!job` asks whether it has a place. A text of the program or none (`const char* reason =
nullptr;`) is two values of the function, the text and whether there is one; the text is read only behind
the check that leaves the block (`if (!reason) continue;`). An array of texts (`const char* const kNames[]
= {...}`) is a list of texts.

A break in the middle of a switch's case leaves the case by a flag, as the break of a loop leaves the loop:
what follows it in the case runs only while the flag is not set.

  tools/cpp_to_blueprint.py --check <namespace> <include flags...> -- <file.cpp>...

only counts: how many functions are written, and what the others wait for.

How a run goes: every source file is read once for the plan of the whole program - which
functions there are, which call which, what the types are called. Then every Blueprint is written
after the Blueprints it calls, with what a call of their functions needs to know; at the end the
libraries of types are put together and all of it is read into the level, a library before the
Blueprints that use it.

Nothing is kept between runs: every run reads the compiler's tree fresh and compiles every test it
writes anew.

Needs clang++ (CLANGXX overrides the binary).
"""
import collections
import codecs
import copy
import io
import itertools
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import traceback

ROOT = os.environ.get("KERN_ROOT") or os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))


class Wait(Exception):
    """What keeps a function C++ for now: something the notation has no place for yet."""


# ---- the notation's words ------------------------------------------------------------------------
CPP_WORDS = set("""alignas alignof and and_eq asm auto bitand bitor bool break case catch char char8_t char16_t char32_t class compl concept const consteval constexpr
constinit const_cast continue co_await co_return co_yield decltype default delete do double dynamic_cast else enum explicit export extern false float for friend goto if inline
int long mutable namespace new noexcept not not_eq nullptr operator or or_eq private protected public register reinterpret_cast requires return short signed sizeof static
static_assert static_cast struct switch template this thread_local throw true try typedef typeid typename union unsigned using virtual void volatile wchar_t while xor xor_eq
Bool Int Float Vector Text Actor U8 U16 U32 I8 I16 I32 List Fixed Callback callback loop machine entry detached kern""".split())   # (kOwnWords, kern/src/logic/notation.cpp)
FORMULA_WORDS = set("min max clamp abs copysign floor ceil round trunc sqrt pow exp expm1 log log10 sin cos tan asin acos atan atan2 lerp hypot fmin fmax fmod isnan isinf isfinite signbit sinh cosh tanh asinh "
                    "num_cast num_bit_cast true false auto".split())
# What nothing of a Blueprint is called: a word of C++ or of the notation, a function of a formula, a node
# (math_add, list_get ... - the engine says which there are: NODES, see main) or a name of the shape the
# notation gives its nodes and paths (n12, go3 ...).
NODES = set()
NODE_SHAPE = re.compile(r"^(n|go|on|it|machine)\d+$")


# --engine <header>: the run writes the engine's own libraries (tools/engine_api.py --libraries) - of each header
# named so, every function and every type a Blueprint can hold, not only what a program calls. The program is
# then a file that only includes them.
ENGINE_RUN = [False]
ENGINE_HEADERS = set()
ENGINE_HEADERS_DIR = os.path.join(ROOT, "kern", "include", "kern")   # (the engine's headers: their types are Blueprints' only where marked)
# A header of the engine that marks a type for Blueprints (KERN_BLUEPRINT_TYPE) is one library of the engine, named by its
# path (kern/input/actions.h: engine_input_actions) - tools/engine_api.py --libraries writes them all into
# kern/src/logic/engine_libraries.gen.cpp. What a Blueprint names of them is checked where it is written (engine_names_missing).
MARKED = re.compile(r"\b(?:struct|class|union|enum(?:\s+class|\s+struct)?)\s+KERN_BLUEPRINT_TYPE\b")
ENGINE_LIBRARIES_GEN = os.path.join(ROOT, "kern", "src", "logic", "engine_libraries.gen.cpp")


def strings_of(x):
    """Every text in a value of lists, tuples and dicts (what a conversion gives: the pieces of a Blueprint's text)."""
    if isinstance(x, str):
        return [x]
    if isinstance(x, dict):
        return [t for v in x.values() for t in strings_of(v)]
    if isinstance(x, (list, tuple, set)):
        return [t for v in x for t in strings_of(v)]
    return []


def marked_headers():
    """The engine's headers (kern/include/kern, without the generated ones and the test's) that mark a type for
    Blueprints: each one library of the engine (relative paths, sorted)."""
    out = []
    for d, _, names in os.walk(ENGINE_HEADERS_DIR):
        for n in names:
            rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.join(d, n), ENGINE_HEADERS_DIR).replace(os.sep, "/")
            if n.endswith(".h") and not n.endswith(".gen.h") and not rel.startswith("test/") and MARKED.search(open(os.path.join(d, n), encoding="utf-8").read()):
                out.append(rel)
    return sorted(out)


def library_of_header(rel):
    """The name of the library of the engine's header `rel` (input/actions.h: engine_input_actions)."""
    return "engine_" + re.sub(r"\W", "_", rel[:-2] if rel.endswith(".h") else rel)


_BUILT = []


def built_engine_libraries():
    """The engine's libraries as the build has them (engine_libraries.gen.cpp): name -> {"types", "functions"} (their own)."""
    if not _BUILT:
        text = open(ENGINE_LIBRARIES_GEN, encoding="utf-8").read() if os.path.exists(ENGINE_LIBRARIES_GEN) else ""
        have = {}
        for m in re.finditer(r'\{"(engine_\w+)", "\w*", R"kl\((.*?)\)kl"\}', text, re.S):
            g = json.loads(m.group(2))
            have[m.group(1)] = {"types": {x["name"] for k in ("structs", "enums") for x in g.get(k, []) if not x.get("from")},
                                "functions": {x["name"] for x in g.get("functions", []) if not x.get("from")}}
        _BUILT.append(have)
    return _BUILT[0]


def engine_libraries_missing():
    """The headers of the engine that mark types and have no library in the build: ["kern/<h>: ..."] (empty: all there)."""
    have = built_engine_libraries()
    return [f"kern/{h} marks types for Blueprints, but the engine has no library {library_of_header(h)} "
            f"(tools/engine_api.py --libraries writes it into {os.path.relpath(ENGINE_LIBRARIES_GEN, ROOT)})"
            for h in marked_headers() if library_of_header(h) not in have]


def engine_names_missing(texts):
    """What Blueprint texts ({who: text}) name of the engine's libraries that the build does not have - a library, or a
    type or function of one: [(who, line, what is missing)] (empty: all there). The one check of it: the converter runs it
    on every text it writes, tools/engine_api.py --check on a folder of texts."""
    have = built_engine_libraries()
    out = []
    for who, text in sorted(texts.items()):
        for at, line in enumerate(text.split("\n"), 1):
            for lib, fn in re.findall(r"\b(engine_\w+?)::(\w+)", line):
                if lib not in have:
                    out.append((who, at, f"{lib}: no library of the engine"))
                elif fn not in have[lib]["types"] and fn not in have[lib]["functions"] and not fn.endswith("Result"):
                    out.append((who, at, f"{lib}: the type or function {fn} - the engine does not write it"))
            for lib in re.findall(r"virtual (engine_\w+)\b", line):
                if lib not in have:
                    out.append((who, at, f"{lib}: no library of the engine"))
    return out




def engine_only(field):
    """A field marked KERN_ENGINE_ONLY (kern/core/base.h): the engine's own, no part of a Blueprint's value."""
    return any(isinstance(c, dict) and c.get("kind") == "AnnotateAttr" for c in field.get("inner", ()))


def is_word(name):
    return name in CPP_WORDS or name in FORMULA_WORDS or name in NODES or bool(NODE_SHAPE.match(name))

NUMBER = {"bool": "bool", "float": "Float", "double": "Float", "long double": "Float", "int": "Int", "long": "Int", "long long": "Int", "short": "Int", "signed char": "Int",
          "unsigned char": "Int", "unsigned short": "Int", "unsigned int": "Int", "char": "Int", "unsigned long": "Int", "unsigned long long": "Int"}
# The number types of the language are those of C++ (kern/docs/blueprint_cpp.md "Zahl-Typen"): each C++ type of a number is
# the Blueprint type of its size and sign - float f32, double f64, int i32, unsigned char u8 ... - nothing is widened.
# (long: 64 bits, as the engine's platforms other than wasm32 hold it - kern/core/base.h names its types by their size.)
EXACT = {"bool": "bool", "float": "f32", "double": "f64", "int": "i32", "long": "i64", "long long": "i64", "short": "i16", "signed char": "i8",
         "unsigned char": "u8", "unsigned short": "u16", "unsigned int": "u32", "char": "i8", "unsigned long": "u64", "unsigned long long": "u64",
         "whole64u": "u64", "whole64s": "i64"}
INTS = ("i8", "i16", "i32", "i64", "u8", "u16", "u32", "u64")
FLOATS = ("f32", "f64")
NUMS = ("bool",) + INTS + FLOATS
NUM_BITS = {"i8": 8, "u8": 8, "i16": 16, "u16": 16, "i32": 32, "u32": 32, "i64": 64, "u64": 64}


def exact_type(t):
    """The Blueprint type of the C++ number type `t` (cleaned), or None: no number type of the language."""
    if t == "long double":
        raise Wait("a long double (the language has f32 and f64)")
    return EXACT.get(t)


def f32_of(x):
    """The f32 nearest the f64 `x` (as C++ converts a double to a float)."""
    try:
        return struct.unpack("f", struct.pack("f", x))[0]
    except OverflowError:
        return float("inf") if x > 0 else float("-inf")


def f32_of_text(text):
    """The f32 C++ makes of the decimal literal `text` (0.05f): the nearest, not the nearest of the nearest double."""
    from fractions import Fraction
    q = Fraction(text)
    if q == 0:
        return 0.0
    sign, q = (-1.0 if q < 0 else 1.0), abs(q)
    e = q.numerator.bit_length() - q.denominator.bit_length()
    if Fraction(2) ** e > q:
        e -= 1
    e = max(e, -126)
    scale = Fraction(2) ** (e - 23)        # (24 bits of mantissa at the exponent e; below 2^-126: the subnormals' step)
    m = q / scale
    whole = m.numerator // m.denominator
    rest = m - whole
    if rest > Fraction(1, 2) or (rest == Fraction(1, 2) and whole % 2):
        whole += 1
    return sign * float(whole * scale) if float(whole * scale) < 3.4028235677973366e38 else sign * float("inf")


def f32_literal(x):
    """An f32 as the notation writes it: the shortest decimal that is this f32, with its f (0.05f)."""
    if x != x or x in (float("inf"), float("-inf")):
        raise Wait("a number that is not finite")
    f = f32_of(x)
    for digits in range(1, 10):
        c = f"{f:.{digits}g}"
        if f32_of(float(c)) == f and f32_of_text(c) == f:
            break
    if "e" not in c and "." not in c:
        c += ".0"
    return c + "f"


def number_literal(x, t):
    """The number `x` as a literal of the language's number type `t` (f32: 0.5f, f64: 0.5, u64: 18446744073709551615u ...)."""
    if t == "bool":
        return "true" if x else "false"
    if t == "f32":
        return f32_literal(x)
    if t == "f64":
        return flit(float(x))
    v = int(x)
    bits = NUM_BITS[t]
    v &= (1 << bits) - 1
    if t[0] == "i" and v >= 1 << (bits - 1):
        v -= 1 << bits
    if t == "i64" and v == -(1 << 63):
        return "-9223372036854775807 - 1"
    return str(v) + ("u" if t == "u64" and v > (1 << 63) - 1 else "")


INT_TEXT = re.compile(r"-?\d+u?")
F32_TEXT = re.compile(r"-?(?:\d+\.\d*|\.\d+|\d+)(?:[eE][+-]?\d+)?f")
F64_TEXT = re.compile(r"-?(?:\d+\.\d*|\.\d+)(?:[eE][+-]?\d+)?|-?\d+[eE][+-]?\d+")


def literal_number(text, t):
    """The number a literal of the language's number type `t` is (as number_literal writes it) - None: `text` is no literal."""
    text = text.strip()
    if t == "bool":
        return {"true": 1, "false": 0}.get(text)
    if t in INTS:
        if text == "-9223372036854775807 - 1":
            return -(1 << 63)
        return int(text.rstrip("u")) if INT_TEXT.fullmatch(text) else None
    if t == "f32":
        return f32_of_text(text[:-1]) if F32_TEXT.fullmatch(text) else None
    if t == "f64":
        return float(text) if F64_TEXT.fullmatch(text) else None
    return None


def number_cast(v, have, want):
    """static_cast<want>(v) of a number `v` of the language's type `have`, as C++ converts it (kern/logic/numbers.h for the
    rest: a whole number to a narrower one keeps its low bits). What C++ leaves undefined - a floating number beyond the
    ends of the whole number type, NaN to a whole number - is a wait, with the reason: the source has to say what it wants."""
    if want == "bool":
        return 1 if v != 0 else 0
    if have == "bool":
        v = 1 if v else 0
    if want in INTS:
        if have in FLOATS:
            if v != v or v in (float("inf"), float("-inf")):
                raise Wait(f"a {have} that is no finite number converted to a {want} (C++ leaves it undefined)")
            v = int(v)   # (toward zero)
            bits, signed = NUM_BITS[want], want[0] == "i"
            lo, hi = (-(1 << (bits - 1)), (1 << (bits - 1)) - 1) if signed else (0, (1 << bits) - 1)
            if not lo <= v <= hi:
                raise Wait(f"a {have} beyond the ends of {want} converted to it (C++ leaves it undefined)")
            return v
        v = int(v) & ((1 << NUM_BITS[want]) - 1)
        if want[0] == "i" and v >= 1 << (NUM_BITS[want] - 1):
            v -= 1 << NUM_BITS[want]
        return v
    if want == "f64":
        return float(v) if have != "f32" else float(v)
    if want == "f32":
        return f32_of_text(str(int(v))) if have in INTS or have == "bool" else f32_of(float(v))
    raise Wait(f"a {have} as a {want}")


def call_parts(text, name):
    """`name<A, B>(x)` spanning all of `text`: ([A, B], x) - else None."""
    if not text.startswith(name + "<"):
        return None
    close = text.find(">(")
    if close < 0:
        return None
    depth = 0
    for i in range(close + 1, len(text)):
        depth += {"(": 1, ")": -1}.get(text[i], 0)
        if depth == 0:
            break
    if depth != 0 or i != len(text) - 1:
        return None
    return [x.strip() for x in text[len(name) + 1:close].split(",")], text[close + 2:-1]


def num_cast(want, have, text):
    """static_cast<want>(x) of the value `text` of the number type `have`: the node Cast, `num_cast<want, have>(x)`
    (kern/docs/blueprint_cpp.md "Zahl-Typen") - every conversion a node of its own. A number written out is the number
    C++ makes of it; a conversion of a whole number widened before is the one of what was widened (the bits are the
    same: num_cast<i64, u64>(num_cast<u64, i32>(i)) is num_cast<i64, i32>(i))."""
    if want == have:
        return text
    v = literal_number(text, have)
    if v is not None:
        return number_literal(number_cast(v, have, want), want)
    inner = call_parts(text, "num_cast")
    if inner is not None and len(inner[0]) == 2 and want in INTS and inner[0][1] in INTS and inner[0][0] in INTS:
        b, c = inner[0]
        if NUM_BITS[b] >= NUM_BITS[c] and NUM_BITS[b] >= NUM_BITS[want]:
            return num_cast(want, c, inner[1])
    return f"num_cast<{want}, {have}>({text})"


# The language's whole number of places, lengths, indexes, codes and ids (what Length, Get Item, Find, Code At, Id of
# Actor take and give): i64. A C++ index of another type (size_t, int) becomes one by its node Cast.
IDX = "i64"
W64 = ("whole64u", "whole64s")     # (a type's name, cleaned: a 64-bit bit pattern, unsigned and signed)
# A map is, in a Blueprint, the list of its rows - structs of a key (`first`) and what it holds (`second`) -,
# a set the list of its keys: in the order they were put in (std::map and std::set: in the order of the
# keys). An iterator into one, or into a list, is the place it stands at - a whole number; the end is the
# list's length.
KEYED = re.compile(r"std::(unordered_)?(multi)?(map|set)<")
# kern::Map, kern::Set (kern/core/map.h): the language's Map and Set - values under keys, keys, in the order the keys came
KEYED_KERN = re.compile(r"kern::(Map|Set)<")
ITERATOR = re.compile(r"std::__detail::_Node_(?:const_)?iterator(?:_base)?<|std::_Rb_tree_(?:const_)?iterator<|__gnu_cxx::__normal_iterator<")
# An iterator that goes through a list from its end (v.rbegin() ... v.rend()) holds, as C++ defines it, a place of
# the list and names the entry before it: that place is what it is here - rbegin() the list's length, rend() 0,
# a step forward one place down.
REVERSE_ITERATOR = re.compile(r"std::reverse_iterator<__gnu_cxx::__normal_iterator<")
